WEBVTT

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.630
BURKE HOLLAND: All right.

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:01.170
Hey everybody.

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:02.160
Welcome back.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.830
And we're joined by Joanna,

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.130
who's the Design
Lead on VS Code.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.170
How many designers
are there on VS Code?

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.220
JOANNA OIKAWA: There are four.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:11.940
BURKE HOLLAND: Four designers.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:12.210
JOANNA OIKAWA: Yes.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:16.410
A small but mighty Design
and UX Engineering Team.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.420
BURKE HOLLAND: Yes.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.310
So interesting back story here.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.620
For those of you who are
here, you get to hear this.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:28.860
There is a UX channel in
the chat, and it's one

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.080
of my favorites, because
you'll see tons of stuff there

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:35.190
that never sees the light of
day, but all the prototypes,

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.890
you're like, I've been thinking,
what if chat looked like this?

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.480
And you're like, that's amazing.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.130
It's one of my favorite
places to hang out

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:43.200
and just watch the
tower building.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:43.710
Very cool.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:43.890
JOANNA OIKAWA: Yeah.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.220
It's great because a lot of
the engineers also own UX.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.530
And so we're able to
have a small team

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.810
and work really quickly,
because everybody is thinking

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.210
and sharing and
owning UX together.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:55.920
BURKE HOLLAND: Yeah,
it's very cool.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.740
I wish I was a designer.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.880
JOANNA OIKAWA: You could.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.530
BURKE HOLLAND: If you
could have one ability

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.570
that you don't have,
what would it be?

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:03.930
JOANNA OIKAWA: Me?

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:04.680
BURKE HOLLAND: Yeah.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.450
JOANNA OIKAWA: Oh, my gosh.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:11.330
Probably print making,
like physical posters

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.490
and graphic design, a
little bit more of that.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.380
BURKE HOLLAND: It
wouldn't be singing,

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.910
Celine Dion or something?

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:17.810
You could have picked anything.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.740
JOANNA OIKAWA: Oh, I could
have picked anything,

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.790
and I picked something
very close to my mom.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:23.720
BURKE HOLLAND: You're
living your best life.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.880
Okay. So almost shipped designs.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.370
Are there a couple of
examples that we can see?

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:29.940
JOANNA OIKAWA: Sure.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.650
Yeah. I mean, I have kind of a
walkthrough of what our journey

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.870
to the agents window looks.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.230
And that includes a lot
of things that are close

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.690
but not quite what we ended
up shipping, and some things

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.940
that we're still thinking about.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.350
So I have, here in Figma, you
can see back in December,

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.650
we were starting to think about
what the evolution of that

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.320
"Agents" panel that we
launched last fall was going

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:55.830
to look like.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.890
How can we make it easier
to switch and check

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:02.370
on your agents while
you're focused in VS Code?

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:06.000
And so we start to play with
this menu that's attached

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.840
to the central
search bar as a way

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.190
to quickly find those things.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.240
And you can see in the product
today, we have a similar menu,

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.900
but we grew and learned
more about the feedback.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.040
And so we started to think more

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.310
about what a full-screen
experience would look like,

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.130
still within the concept
of the same editor window,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.020
but we started with
the welcome page.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.420
And we ended up shipping
something close to this,

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.990
but one of the key
differences was what kind

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:35.760
of information we
wanted to show here.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.430
And so this took a lot of
feedback, user research,

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:42.570
to learn about what data

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:45.450
across what workspaces do
you want to see things.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.690
And we really learned that a lot
of folks wanted to see sessions

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.880
across different projects.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.170
BURKE HOLLAND: How do
you make that decision?

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.820
Is it just looking at telemetry?

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.810
I mean, this seems like
this stuff is hard.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.130
It's extremely hard.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.800
And when you see products that
are really well designed,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.480
they're just easy to use, that
is extremely difficult to do.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.330
So I'm very curious about, like,
how do you figure out which one

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.880
of these designs is the
right one to go with?

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.500
JOANNA OIKAWA: It's a challenge
because we're working

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.410
in an existing product
with existing patterns

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.630
and preferences and then trying

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.540
to support new ways
of working entirely.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:21.870
BURKE HOLLAND: Yeah.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:23.940
Modalities that we don't really
know what they look like.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.310
JOANNA OIKAWA: And so we're
learning about them on our team.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:29.340
And our customers, our users
are learning about them.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:34.350
And it's kind of building
the boat while it's running

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:34.980
type thing.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.000
BURKE HOLLAND: Yeah,
we're changing the wheels

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.020
on the bus while it's -- so who
makes the final call on that?

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:40.620
Is that you?

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.040
Are you the one who says
ship it or don't ship it?

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.230
JOANNA OIKAWA: We're always
kind of improving on it,

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:49.290
so there's not
usually a final call.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.240
We'll put it into
insiders first.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.110
We'll experiment.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:52.860
BURKE HOLLAND: Oh, that's right.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:53.340
Test rollout.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:54.120
JOANNA OIKAWA:
We'll run research.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.020
We'll get feedback.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.330
And then we're just kind of
continuously touching a lot

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:00.150
of these problem spaces
as we learn more,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:03.060
as our users learn more.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.070
No one really calls
it final on the team.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.590
We just kind of keep going
until we're happy with it.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:08.460
BURKE HOLLAND: Okay.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.790
And y'all have something
called UI Power Hour.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:12.930
UI Power Hour.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:13.740
Is that a thing?

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:14.820
JOANNA OIKAWA: I don't know.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.560
BURKE HOLLAND: It says
what it is, how it works,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.860
and why it matters
at this velocity.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.640
JOANNA OIKAWA: I have no idea.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.860
BURKE HOLLAND: The AI
has hallucinated facts.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:30.840
AI designers shipping their
own fixes, oh, with Figma MCP.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:30.930
Nice.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:32.250
JOANNA OIKAWA: Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:38.040
We have taken advantage of the
capability to take designs

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.130
from Figma, just like
this, and move them

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:45.650
into code more quickly and,
more importantly, take code

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:48.020
and update our Figma components,
so that you can work

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:51.620
in either tool, whatever
is easiest for you.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:54.770
And all of our designers
ship code as well.

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:55.580
And so we --

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:56.090
BURKE HOLLAND: Oh, really?

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:57.410
Are you submitting PRs?

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:58.410
JOANNA OIKAWA: Yes.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.440
BURKE HOLLAND: Now is that a
change from 12 months ago,

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.630
or has it always been that way?

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.610
JOANNA OIKAWA: A lot
of former designers,

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.430
like David Dossett, have
been very integrated

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:09.660
in the code base as well.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.230
But it's kind of a
new thing for us.

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:16.260
So we're finding that balance
of when to submit PRs,

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.890
when to just create prototypes,
when to just work in Figma,

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:21.300
what's most efficient.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.660
We have a lot of web-based
prototypes as well that we use.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:29.820
This is from last December as
well when we were starting

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.030
to talk about our new
themes and color refreshes

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.430
and what were some of
the experimental styles

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:36.870
that we wanted to play with.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:41.880
And so prototyping these to make
them interactive was excellent.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.860
And we didn't end up
shipping this exact code,

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.030
but it gave us
something to share

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:46.800
with the team, gather feedback.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.800
And you can see some of the
options that we had here,

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:53.610
like the shadow on the panels,
the lighter panels than the rest

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.770
of the background,
these are things

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:56.550
that we ended up changing.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:57.600
BURKE HOLLAND: The VS Code icon

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.120
in the top right
corner was flashing.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:00.780
I like that.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.150
JOANNA OIKAWA: Yeah.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.550
The evolution of this
is the "Update" button

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.440
that lives up there now.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.440
But we got feedback that the
flashing button was a little

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:10.800
too much.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:11.610
BURKE HOLLAND: A
little too annoying?

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.230
JOANNA OIKAWA: Yeah.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.580
So maybe we'll just
add it for you.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.080
BURKE HOLLAND: I really like it.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.260
Okay. So what I'm hearing,
Joanna, because I heard this

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:25.050
from Courtney as well, is
that everybody is now --

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.840
would it be accurate to say
that everyone is a developer?

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:30.960
In other words, PMs
are submitting PRs.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.290
Designers are submitting PRs.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:37.380
So that, to me, seems
to be a huge change.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:40.110
The job has really expanded
beyond just design.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.010
JOANNA OIKAWA: Everyone
is a developer,

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:43.590
and everyone is a
designer as well.

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:45.120
Like I said, a lot
of our engineers --

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:45.630
BURKE HOLLAND: That's very true.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:48.690
JOANNA OIKAWA: -- they're able
to come up with new ideas

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:50.670
and get farther in the UX
process than they would have

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:55.380
in the past because they
have more design guidelines

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.930
that are available, and the code
can look at what's happened

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:00.990
in the past, and they can bring
a prototype to us for feedback.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:01.260
BURKE HOLLAND: Yeah,
that's very true.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.360
JOANNA OIKAWA: And so
we get to share a lot

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.250
of responsibilities
because of these tools.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:06.750
BURKE HOLLAND: It moves
in both directions.

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:11.820
I'm curious, do y'all think
that maybe in the future,

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:15.420
the job is you do everything?

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:16.440
Do you know what I mean?

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.320
You're a designer and
a PM and an engineer.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.840
Everyone literally
does everything.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.500
We don't have siloed
disciplines anymore.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:26.220
Or no?

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:27.990
JOANNA OIKAWA: The
way I think about it

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:29.460
and the way our team thinks

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.280
about it is depth
versus breadth.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.440
And so my depth will
probably always be

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.660
in user experience
and visual design.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:40.500
And that's where my deep
expertise comes in.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:44.790
But with AI, you can have more
breadth than in the past.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:48.750
So, for instance, writing
a Kusto query used

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.390
to take me forever, and
it was really cumbersome

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:52.410
and difficult to do.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:56.370
But now I can look up
data on our telemetry

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:58.710
and make decisions off
of that much easier.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.820
So I can put on that
analyst hat, PM hat,

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.220
even though I don't have
that extreme depth.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:07.350
BURKE HOLLAND: So for
those folks who don't work

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:10.470
in Microsoft, Kusto is, well,

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:11.850
Kuso is a public
thing, too, I think.

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:15.450
But Kusto is a language for
querying telemetry and data.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:17.400
And Microsoft is
very data driven.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.950
Right? It doesn't just
make decisions on the fly.

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:21.360
Everything has to
be backed by data.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.620
So we used to have people
whose sole job was

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:28.500
to write Kusto queries
and then make dashboards

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:29.400
and present that data.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.020
That was Devon.

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:31.740
That's what he did.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.740
And if you needed data,
you'd have to go to Devon,

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.720
because he's the
only one who knew.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.330
But I guess now just
anyone could do it.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.580
JOANNA OIKAWA: We're able
to get farther on our own.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:42.120
BURKE HOLLAND: Holy crap.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.140
This place is coming apart.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.910
As far as design, though,
goes with agents,

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:49.290
one of the things
I've noticed is

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:52.080
that we talk about AI psychosis.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.440
And one of the ways that it
manifests is that now I think

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:56.820
that I'm a good designer.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:58.470
And so the AI makes the design.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:08:59.910
I'm like, I really like that.

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:01.860
And other people will
show me their thing.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:05.550
They're like, I built this
with Opus or whatever,

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.520
and they're super proud of
it, and it's just purple

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.370
and gradients all
over the place.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.370
I'm like, that is a monstrosity.

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:18.660
So it does seem like, yes, you
can do, but also there needs

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.110
to be someone in the
room who's like,

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:22.640
we're not going to do that.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:23.480
Close.

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:25.040
JOANNA OIKAWA: That's
where the depth

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:29.060
and the taste comes in,
having folks, and, similarly,

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:32.780
having engineers who can
tell me that's not possible,

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:34.850
or that's not smart, or it's
going to slow down the app,

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:36.980
when I have this fun idea.

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:39.770
Having these complementary
depths really helps find the

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:40.850
right balance for the product.

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:41.960
BURKE HOLLAND: So I know
we're kind of off the rails

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:48.230
of design, but I'm interested
in this, because if you're old

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:51.290
like me, I've been
around for so long

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:53.060
that I've just seen
everything, right?

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:56.610
So it's like taste and
experience is more of just,

00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:59.820
like, I've just done every
stupid thing you can do.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:01.830
And so I know how it all ends.

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:04.080
I've burned myself
every possible way.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:06.190
I'm just covered in blisters.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:12.300
How do we develop the
taste if you don't get

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:16.350
to get burned anymore because
the agent is doing all the work?

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:20.280
I'm worried about how do we
create more of these folks?

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:22.950
JOANNA OIKAWA: That's why
having a validation loop

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:27.630
and making sure what you're
shipping is solving the problem

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:30.420
that you intended, and
you're getting feedback,

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:31.920
and you're kind of
closing the loop

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.140
on decisions that you're making.

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:37.260
That's how you'll actually
find out is this little thing

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.280
that I vibe coded,
added really quickly,

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:41.660
is it actually
solving the problem,

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:45.770
or does it have a greater sense
of importance and permanence

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:48.620
because it's out
there and available?

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:52.560
So holding ourselves
responsible for making sure

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.660
that the things we ship
work and taking things

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:57.420
out when it doesn't work,
because it's so easy

00:10:57.420 --> 00:10:59.280
to just add, add, add, add, add.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:00.360
BURKE HOLLAND: Yeah,
it's fascinating.

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:01.650
It is easy to do.

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:03.630
I almost feel like we're at
the point, especially with AI,

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:06.210
where it's like, I don't
know if y'all have ever --

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:09.990
are there any artists in the
room, people who paint or draw?

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:12.420
If you draw, then you know
that when you're drawing,

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:16.650
there is a point at which
you should stop drawing.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:19.140
From here on out, you're
just going to make it worse.

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:21.570
And I feel like that's
the same way with AI.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:24.690
You can build every
possible feature,

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:27.540
but you should really
stop and consider whether

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:28.680
or not you should do that.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:29.580
JOANNA OIKAWA: And
that's been a big theme

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:32.940
with the Agents Window, because
we want to provide a place

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:36.840
where you can focus
and easily navigate

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:40.020
and complement the craziness

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:43.080
of running multiple
agents in parallel.

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:45.330
And that requires kind
of pulling back things

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:49.320
that historically might have
been an option in VS Code.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:54.360
And so I'll even show in Figma
here what that started to look

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:58.080
when we realized we needed kind
of a separate window in a space

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:02.370
to look across multiple projects
and have that focused view.

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:05.220
We really questioned every
single element on the screen

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:06.630
of whether it was going to be --

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:07.710
BURKE HOLLAND:
Distracting or necessary.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:09.180
JOANNA OIKAWA: -- needed.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:11.430
Does it help me make
decisions in this moment,

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:15.180
or does it add more information
and options and distractions?

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:17.940
And so we played a lot with
what the output looks like,

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:20.430
what the session list
information needs to include,

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:24.300
and then even at this level,
what information do I need

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.940
to start my day to
check on things,

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:29.400
to babysit multiple agents at
a time, and how do we balance

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:31.650
that with not making
people feel overwhelmed

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:34.290
and like there's too many
things happening at once

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:36.150
that it's out of their control.

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:39.450
BURKE HOLLAND: Well, I, on
behalf of all developers,

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:41.550
am thankful for designers

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:44.130
who can help us ship
good-looking stuff.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:45.180
Thank you so much.

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:47.490
A round of applause for Joanna.

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:48.210
JOANNA OIKAWA: Thanks
for having me.

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:49.230
BURKE HOLLAND: You're
my favorite designer.

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:50.790
Don't tell Elijah I said that.

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:51.150
All right.

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:51.600
Thanks so much.

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:53.220
All right, folks,
we'll be right back.

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:54.780
We're going to do Doom next.

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:56.520
I don't know how.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:59.920
[ Music ]
